
[38;5;231m⏺[39m Browser it is — [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m.

  Next: right now nothing in the app is configurable, so "settings" has no referent yet. That's a real fork, not a
  detail:

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this change ships user-visible settings (which means new UI in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and wiring into the login
  flow) or only the storage layer that later features read and write.

  - [1mStorage layer only[0m — a [38;5;153mpreferences[39m module with get/set/remove/clear over [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, defaults, and JSON
    serialization. No UI. Buys a clean, testable foundation; costs having nothing observable to demo, and the API's
    shape is guessed rather than driven by a real consumer.
  - [1mStorage + one concrete setting[0m — the layer plus "remember my username", which prefills the login field on return
    visits. Buys a real consumer that validates the API, and it's visibly working. Costs one checkbox in [38;5;153mindex.html[39m and
    a small change to the submit handler.
  - [1mStorage + a settings panel[0m — the layer plus several settings (remember username, theme, etc.) and a UI to edit them.
    Buys a complete feature; costs the most code, and most of it is UI unrelated to storage.

  [1mMy recommendation: storage + "remember username".[0m One real consumer is what keeps the API honest — a storage layer
  with no caller tends to grow options nobody needs. It's also the setting the existing login form actually implies. A
  panel can come later once there's more than one thing to put in it.

  One caveat worth stating plainly: [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m is readable by any script on the origin, so the username is fine to
  store but the [1mpassword must never be[0m. I'll make that an explicit constraint in the spec.
